meta {
  name: Address Autocomplete
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/address/autocomplete
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

script:pre-request {
  // One token for the whole address-entry session: these keystrokes plus the
  // Place Details call in `Resolve Address` are billed as one session only if
  // both carry the same value. Minted here, consumed there.
  //
  // Hand-rolled rather than `crypto.randomUUID()` — Bruno's script sandbox is
  // QuickJS and has no `require`. The API only asks for 8-64 opaque characters.
  bru.setVar(
    "addressSessionToken",
    "bru-" +
      Math.random().toString(36).slice(2) +
      Math.random().toString(36).slice(2),
  );
}

body:json {
  {
    "input": "4821 North Maple",
    "sessionToken": "{{addressSessionToken}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  // Capture a real place id for `Resolve Address` when a key is configured.
  //
  // Falls back to a sentinel rather than "" so the next request still exercises
  // the service: an empty placeId would be rejected by the zod pipe and never
  // reach `AddressService`, hiding the unconfigured 200 that is the whole point
  // of the fail-open contract.
  const first = (res.getBody().suggestions || [])[0];
  bru.setVar("addressPlaceId", first ? first.placeId : "no-key-configured");
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK", function () {
    // Always 200 — this endpoint fails open. See the docs block.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("carries the { available, suggestions } envelope", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body).to.have.property("available");
    expect(body.available).to.be.a("boolean");
    expect(body.suggestions).to.be.an("array");
  });

  test("every suggestion is clickable and human-readable", function () {
    for (const s of res.getBody().suggestions) {
      expect(s.placeId).to.be.a("string").and.not.equal("");
      expect(s.primaryText).to.be.a("string").and.not.equal("");
      expect(s).to.have.property("secondaryText");
    }
  });

  test("never returns raw Google fields or anything key-shaped", function () {
    const raw = JSON.stringify(res.getBody());
    // The proxy exists so the browser never sees Google's payload or key.
    expect(raw).to.not.include("AIza");
    expect(raw).to.not.include("addressComponents");
    expect(raw).to.not.include("googleapis.com");
  });

  test("rejects an input too short to be worth a billed request", function () {
    // Documented here rather than as a second request: the 3-character floor is
    // a cost control, not a UX rule, and it belongs next to the happy path.
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /address/autocomplete — Google-backed address predictions (PAC-60)

  Type-ahead behind every address field in the app: the New Lead household
  address, the per-policy property address on the Lead and Quote Recap policy
  drawers, and the Sold wizard's escrow card. Restores what SmartSuite's native
  `address` field did for free before the rebuild.

  - **Module:** any of `leads`, `quote_recaps`, `deal_audits`, `crm_service`
  - **Permission:** any of `leads:write`, `quote_recaps:write`,
    `deal_audits:write`, `crm_service:write`
  - **Data scope:** none — the response is third-party data derived from what the
    caller just typed, and touches no tenant record.

  > **Why an OR gate over four modules, and no `ModuleKey.Address`?** Address
  > entry is not owned by one page. A producer types one on New Lead (`leads`),
  > a Quote Recap author on the policy drawer (`quote_recaps`), a producer again
  > in the Sold wizard (`deal_audits`), and a CSR in Policy Transfer
  > (`crm_service`) — and a CSR holds no `deal_audits` at all. Gating on any
  > single key would 403 at least one of them, on a control whose only job is to
  > save typing.
  >
  > A dedicated module key was rejected for the same reason `GET /carriers` has
  > none: `PAGES` is one entry per module key, so it would add a row to the
  > permission matrix for something that is not a page, and hand every agency a
  > switch that silently breaks address entry on four forms.
  >
  > `write`, not `read` — this exists to help someone *fill in* a form.

  ## Why POST and not GET
  A partially-typed home address in a query string lands in the API access log,
  in every proxy along the path, and in whatever aggregates them. The body keeps
  it out of all three.

  ## Request
  ```json
  {
    "input": "4821 North Maple",
    "sessionToken": "3519edfe-0f75-4a30-bfe4-7cbd89340b2c"
  }
  ```

  | Field | Rule |
  |---|---|
  | `input` | 3–200 chars, trimmed. Below three, predictions return everything and help nobody — the floor is a cost control, and the client debounces on top of it. |
  | `sessionToken` | 8–64 chars, opaque. Bounded rather than pattern-matched: we mint UUIDs, but the web client has a fallback generator for non-secure contexts (`crypto.randomUUID` is undefined over plain http on a LAN address), and pinning the format would break the day it fires. |

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  {
    "available": true,
    "suggestions": [
      {
        "placeId": "ChIJ...",
        "primaryText": "4821 N Maple Ave",
        "secondaryText": "Oklahoma City, OK, USA"
      }
    ]
  }
  ```

  Predictions are restricted to `street_address` in the `US` — businesses and
  cities are noise in a field that wants a mailing address, and non-US addresses
  are out of scope for PAC-60.

  ## ⚠ This endpoint fails open — read `available`, not the status
  **It is always `200`.** A missing API key, a revoked key, an API not enabled on
  the GCP project, a timeout and a Google outage all come back `200` with an
  empty `suggestions` array; `available` is what distinguishes them:

  | `available` | Meaning | What the client does |
  |---|---|---|
  | `true`, suggestions present | normal | render the dropdown |
  | `true`, suggestions empty | nothing matched, or a transient blip | no dropdown this keystroke, keep asking |
  | `false` | no key, revoked key, API not enabled | stop asking for the rest of the page session; the field degrades to a plain text input |

  Throwing `503` was considered and rejected: this fires on a debounced
  keystroke, so it would mean an error toast per keystroke and a log line per
  keystroke for a feature whose entire job is to save typing. Temperament-wise
  this sits with the permission cache (degrade quietly, the request still works)
  rather than the mail transport (shout, because the email is gone).

  **The integration can never block a submission** — an explicit PAC-60
  acceptance criterion, and it is held structurally rather than by convention:
  no submit path in this API calls Google, and nothing here writes to form
  state or adds a validation rule. A rural or new-build address Google has never
  heard of still submits.

  ## Billing
  The `sessionToken` bundles the keystrokes of one address entry with the
  terminating `POST /address/resolve`. Note the first 12 autocomplete requests
  in a session are still billed individually — only requests beyond the 12th
  fall under the free session SKU — so the client's 250 ms debounce is a real
  cost lever, not just a UX one.

  Spend is capped in three layers: a quota cap on the Google key (the only hard
  stop — budgets merely alert), this route's per-IP throttle, and, on the public
  share-link variant, a per-link daily cap.

  ## ⚠ Tests assert the envelope, never real predictions
  A dev machine will usually have **no `GOOGLE_MAPS_API_KEY`**, so this request
  answers `{ "available": false, "suggestions": [] }` and that is a pass.
  Asserting that predictions come back would make the collection red for
  everyone without a key, which is most of the team.

  ## Rate limiting
  `120 requests / minute / IP` by default (`ADDRESS_LOOKUP_RATE_LIMIT`).
  ⚠ Must be set as a **real environment variable** — `@Throttle` bakes the
  number into decorator metadata when the controller class is imported, which is
  before `ConfigModule` runs dotenv. A value in `.env` is silently ignored.

  ## Errors
  - `400 Bad Request` — `input` shorter than 3 characters, or a missing/oversized
    `sessionToken`.
  - `401 Unauthorized` — missing/expired access token.
  - `403 Forbidden` — none of the four modules enabled, or none of the four write
    permissions held.
  - `429 Too Many Requests` — rate limit exceeded.

  ## Why this lives under `Leads` and not in a folder of its own
  Same reason as activity logging: the CLI walks folders alphabetically, and a
  top-level `Address` folder would sort **before `Auth`** and run with no
  `accessToken`.

  ## Run order
  Needs only `Auth/Login`. Sets `addressSessionToken` (consumed by
  `Resolve Address`) and `addressPlaceId` (empty when no key is configured).
}
